You are the rubric judge for one run of an agent on a task. Everything you need is in your working directory; read it rather than guess:

- `instruction.md`: the task the agent was given.
- `final_message.md`: the agent's final message to the user, its answer.
- `workspace/`: the files the agent delivered. Read-only for you: create, edit or delete nothing.
- `end_env.json`, when present: a snapshot of every application's records after the agent finished, as the servers handed them back.
- the agent's trajectory, at the path given at the end of this prompt: every step, tool call and tool result, in order.

Judge each criterion below as a yes/no question about this run only. A criterion tagged [final answer] is judged on `final_message.md`, and on the delivered files when it refers to them. One tagged [trajectory] is judged on what the agent actually did, as the trajectory records it. One tagged [end state] is judged on the records in `end_env.json`, with the trajectory's write calls as supporting evidence; without `end_env.json` it is judged on those write calls alone. Answer "yes" only when the evidence is explicit: a claim the agent merely implies, or a figure that is close but not the one the criterion states, is "no". In your reasoning quote the evidence and say where it came from (the file, or the step number). Do not re-solve the task, do not run the agent's code, and do not change any file.

- 'r-s13-released-revision': [final answer] The reply explains that S13 cites the risk management report DOC-1047 at Rev F rather than Rev E (the revision in Ines's working draft) because Rev F has been released into the controlled store and is now the version in force. Page counts are not required, and it does not matter whether the reply describes this as a change that happened during the work. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-s14-shared-document': [final answer] The reply explains that S14 (benefit-risk determination) also cites DOC-1047 Rev F rather than Rev E, because S14 is satisfied by the same document as S13. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-s22-released-revision': [final answer] The reply explains that S22 cites the stability report DOC-1188 at Rev C rather than Rev B (the revision in Ines's working draft) because Rev C has been released into the controlled store and is now the version in force. Page counts are not required, and it does not matter whether the reply describes this as a change that happened during the work. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-change-request-evidence': [final answer] The reply identifies change request CHG0041187, closed as implemented, as the record that released DOC-1047 Rev F and DOC-1188 Rev C and so makes them the versions in force. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-page-total-counts-releases': [final answer] In explaining how it arrived at the page total, the reply makes clear that the total counts the newly released revisions DOC-1047 Rev F and DOC-1188 Rev C - for example by giving their page counts in the breakdown, by saying the total includes them, or by saying that they add 13 pages to the earlier figure. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-register-lag-reasoning': [final answer] The reply explains that DOC-1047 Rev F and DOC-1188 Rev C count as in force even though the Document Control Register does not list them yet, because the standard or QA-SOP-007 accepts a release under a change request closed as implemented as approval evidence until the register is reconciled. No rule or clause number is required. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-s22-no-open-placeholder': [final answer] The reply gives as the reason S22 is Complete and not a gap (unlike Ines's working draft) that DOC-1188 Rev C records no open placeholders, the open placeholders of Rev B having been closed. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-register-follow-up': [final answer] Among the follow-ups for document control, the reply says that the register still has to record DOC-1047 Rev F and DOC-1188 Rev C (for example at the next register sync). (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-ifu-approval-completed': [final answer] The reply explains that S07 cites the professional IFU DOC-1133 at Rev G rather than Rev F (the revision in Ines's working draft) because the approval of Rev G has completed (the register shows it Approved, or its approval envelope is completed). It does not matter whether the reply describes this as a change that happened during the work. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-not-applicable-reasons': [final answer] For the not-applicable sections, the reply gives each one's reason: S08 and S26 apply to self-testing devices only, S27 to companion diagnostics only, S28 to class D devices only. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-lookalikes-stay-in': [final answer] The reply explains that S25 stays applicable because near-patient testing by health professionals is not self-testing, and that S30 stays applicable because Edition 3 requires the summary of safety and performance for class C devices. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-s15-routed-revision': [final answer] The reply explains that S15 cites DOC-1102 Rev C because Rev D is still in approval (not approved) in the register. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-s20-change-request-release': [final answer] The reply explains that S20 cites DOC-1111 Rev C because Rev C was released under a change request closed as implemented, although the register still shows Rev B as the approved revision. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-s17-usability-number': [final answer] The reply explains that S17 departs from Ines's working draft because the usability engineering file is DOC-1074, while DOC-1047 (the number in the draft) is the risk management report. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-s16-placeholder-gap': [final answer] The reply gives as the reason S16 is a gap that DOC-1103 Rev C, although approved, records an open placeholder. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-s23-draft-only': [final answer] The reply gives as the reason S23 is a gap that DOC-1120 exists only as a working draft, so no version is in force. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-s29-withdrawn-approval': [final answer] The reply gives as the reason S29 is a gap that the approval of DOC-1200 Rev A was withdrawn (the CAPA number is not required), so no version is in force. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-shared-document-counted-once': [final answer] In explaining the page total, the reply says that DOC-1047 is counted once although both S13 and S14 cite it. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-index-left-out': [final answer] The reply names the glossary terms left out of the index with a reason for each: Reagent pack and Measuring interval because they are retired, Residual risk acceptability matrix because it is only proposed, and Lay user and Self-test result because their source documents are not cited by any applicable section. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-pen-working-draft-total': [final answer] The reply gives 828 pages (the total of Ines's working draft) as the page total of the technical file. Quoting 828 only as the working draft's own total, for example in a comparison of the draft with the reply's total, is not this. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-pen-total-before-release': [final answer] The reply gives 771 pages as the technical file's final page total. Mentioning 771 only as the total before the late releases is not this. (score: "yes" or "no")
- 'r-pen-ssp-excluded': [final answer] The reply says that S30 (summary of safety and performance) is not applicable to the Kestrel-3. Reporting only that Ines's working draft or someone else marked S30 not applicable, while the reply itself keeps S30 applicable, is not this. (score: "yes" or "no")

